.TH MPF-GUI 1 "2026-11-19" "MPF 3.11.1 (6daa0e50)" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mpf-gui \- dump a disc and write its submission info, from a desktop window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mpf-gui
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mpf-gui
(upstream \fBMPF.Avalonia\fR) is the desktop program of the Media
Preservation Frontend (MPF). You pick the drive, the system and the
dumping program in its window; it starts that program, shows its output,
and then, in the same process, reads the dump and its logs and writes the
Redump submission information (\fI!submissionInfo.txt\fR) next to the
output, the same step
.BR mpf-check (1)
performs on its own.
.PP
The \fBTools\fR menu holds \fBCheck Dump\fR, which runs that step on a dump
made earlier, \fBCreate PS3 IRD\fR, and \fBOptions\fR, where all settings
live.
.B mpf-gui
takes no command-line options. The desktop entry starts it from the
application menu.
.SH DISPLAY
MPF builds this program on Avalonia 11.3, which has an X11 backend only.
On a Wayland session it runs through Xwayland, which the common desktops
install; on an X11 session it runs directly. The X11 libraries it loads are
recommended packages, not required ones, so that the other MPF packages
stay small on machines without a display; install them if your system
leaves recommended packages out.
.SH CONFIGURATION
MPF reads
.I ~/.config/mpf/config.json
when it exists, and only otherwise a
.I config.json
next to the binary. The
.B mpf-gui
wrapper creates the file in your home before the first start, so that is
the one in use. It sets the four dumping-program paths to the names of the
packaged programs, which MPF finds on
.BR PATH :
.BR redumper-mpf ,
.BR aaru5 ,
.B DiscImageCreator.out
(package \fBdiscimagecreator\fR) and
.BR dreamdump .
.B mpf-gui
recommends these packages but does not require them; install the ones you
want to dump with.
The wrapper sets a path back to that name only when the configured one no
longer resolves, so a path of your own stays as long as it exists.
.PP
On the first start MPF opens its options window; closing it writes the
complete configuration. From then on
.BR mpf-check (1)
reads the file too, where before it used its own defaults.
.PP
.B redumper-mpf
and
.B aaru5
are the builds this MPF build was made for. The rolling
.B redumper
and
.B aaru
packages may be newer, and MPF does not check the version of the program it
starts.
.SH OUTPUT PATH
Upstream MPF defaults
.B DefaultOutputPath
to the relative directory
.IR ISO ,
which resolves against the directory
.B mpf-gui
was started from. The wrapper sets it to
.I ~/ISO
instead, and resets it whenever it is empty or relative. Any absolute path
you set under \fBTools\fR \(-> \fBOptions\fR stays. The path may contain
path macros such as \fB%SYSTEM%\fR or \fB%DATE%\fR;
.B mpf-cli listpath
lists them.
.SH DRIVE ACCESS
.B mpf-gui
runs unprivileged and carries no file capabilities: it sends no drive
commands itself. The dumping program does, and
.BR redumper-mpf ,
.B aaru5
and
.B DiscImageCreator.out
are installed with the
.B cap_sys_rawio
file capability, which the kernel grants from the executed file no matter
who starts it.
.B dreamdump
is installed without it.
Read access to
.I /dev/sr*
comes from the
.B uaccess
rule at a local desktop seat; in a session without one add yourself to the
.B cdrom
group:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo usermod \-aG cdrom $USER
.RE
.PP
The GUI must
.B not
be given file capabilities, and this package deliberately does not. A
process that has them runs non-dumpable, which makes
.I /proc/<pid>/root
root-owned;
.B xdg-desktop-portal
reads exactly that path to identify the calling application, fails, and
refuses the request with "Portal operation not allowed". Every file
dialog then dies with an unhandled D-Bus exception and takes the program
with it.
.SH OLDER SYSTEMS
MPF reads and writes its log archives (\fI*_logs.zip\fR) through a library
that needs glibc 2.34 or newer. Below that (Enterprise Linux 8 has 2.28),
compressing the logs fails and leaves an empty archive, and extracting an
archive writes every file from it as 0 bytes, over any copy already in the
folder, while MPF still reports success. There the wrapper turns
.B CompressLogFiles
off in the configuration at every start. Leave "Compress Log Files" off
under \fBTools\fR \(-> \fBOptions\fR: the wrapper turns it off again at the
next start, but a dump in the same session would leave an empty archive.
The wrapper cannot see the paths you pick in the window, so do not run
\fBCheck Dump\fR on a dump with a \fI_logs.zip\fR next to it; extract the
archive with
.B unzip
and move it out of the folder first.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/mpf-gui
The wrapper described above; it starts the binary below.
.TP
.I /usr/lib64/mpf-gui/MPF.Avalonia
The self-contained .NET binary.
.TP
.I /usr/share/applications/mpf-gui.desktop
The desktop entry.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mpf/config.json
The configuration, shared with
.BR mpf-cli (1)
and
.BR mpf-check (1).
.SH NOTES
This page is written by hand against MPF 3.11.1 commit 6daa0e50. MPF is
packaged as a rolling build, so the installed program may be newer.
